# SQL NULL Handling

Scanning a NULL DB value into a Go primitive (`int`, `string`) causes a runtime panic.

## Rule

Every nullable DB column MUST be scanned into the corresponding `sql.Null*` type:

| DB Type | Go Scan Type | Extract |
|---------|-------------|---------|
| VARCHAR NULL | `sql.NullString` | `if v.Valid { x = v.String }` |
| INT NULL | `sql.NullInt64` | `if v.Valid { x = int(v.Int64) }` |
| BOOLEAN NULL | `sql.NullBool` | `if v.Valid { x = v.Bool }` |
| TIMESTAMP NULL | `sql.NullTime` | `if v.Valid { x = v.Time }` |

## Known Nullable Columns

- `routes.port` — use `sql.NullInt64`
- `routes.username`, `routes.password`, `routes.api_key` — use `sql.NullString`
- `routes.tls_mode`, `routes.status`, `routes.protocol_type` — use `sql.NullString`
- `rules.client_id` — use `sql.NullInt64` (NULL = global rule)
- `rules.tag` — use `sql.NullString`
- `rules.updated_at` — use `sql.NullTime`

## Example

```go
var port sql.NullInt64
err := rows.Scan(&port)
if port.Valid {
    entry.Port = int(port.Int64)
}
```
